    "item-handbooktitle-pressedfruitmash": "Mash Enhancements (Expanded Foods)",
    "item-handbooktext-pressedfruitmash": "Simmer fruit mash in a saucepan with sweetener and water to get started on making <a href=\"handbook://item-expandedfoods:fruitleather-wet\">fruit leather</a>, a sweet and gummy fruit snack.",

    "game:item-handbooktitle-fruitpress": "Fruitpress notes",
    "game:item-handbooktext-fruitpress": "You can only use the fruit press if it is at LEAST half-full. Anything less and the press will not squish any material. Be sure to check and see if your mash can be used for anything!",
  
    "item-handbooktitle-alcoholagingguide": "Alcohol Aging Guide (Expanded Foods)",
    "item-handbooktext-alcoholagingguide": "All alcohols now cure over time. If it finishes curing before it begins to perish into vinegar, then it progresses to the next stage, becoming more intoxicating and filling.<br>Ciders and ales (usually created from curing juice or sealing it in a barrel) cure into 'strong' alcohol, then into 'fine' alcohol.<br>Spirits, such as brandy and whiskey (created by distilling cider/ale in a <a href=\"handbook://block-verticalboiler-west\">boiler</a>), cure into 'aged' alcohol, then into 'vintage' alcohol.<br>'Fine' alcohol can still spoil if improperly stored - make sure it's stored someplace cool so it can 'cure' into itself instead of spoiling into vinegar. 'Vintage' alcohol lasts indefinitely, due to the concentrated alcohol content.",
  
    "item-handbooktitle-fryingguide": "Frying Guide",
    "item-handbooktext-fryingguide": "Almost anything can be fried, if you put your mind to it. Get started by taking a basic ingredient, such as cheese, vegetables, meat, or boiled eggs, then <a href=\"handbook://kneadingmixingguide\">knead</a> it with a bonding agent such as food oil, honey, lard, liquid egg, or syrup, some flour to form a mild crust, and a handful of bread crumbs to finalize the breading. This breaded item can be further kneaded with lard or oil to enhance its flavor, then baked in an oven- but it may burn if left unattended. <a href=\"handbook://simmeringguide\">Simmering</a> it with lard or oil will result in a perfectly fried food every time, and cauldrons make it easy to fry in bulk.",
  
    "item-handbooktitle-juiceprocessingguide": "Juice Processing Guide (Expanded Foods)",
    "item-handbooktext-juiceprocessingguide": "Fruit juice is a base liquid with many resulting outputs.<br>If allowed to spoil, it ferments into <a href=\"handbook://item-vinegarportion\">vinegar</a> and acidifies.<br>When sealed into a barrel or <a href=\"handbook://block-aculinaryartillery:bottle-clay-blue-corked\">bottle</a>, juice cures over time into 'cider' (the exact name varies based on the fruit used).<br>Lastly, juice may be <a href=\"handbook://simmeringguide\">simmered</a> in a <a href=\"handbook://block-aculinaryartillery:saucepan-blue-fired\">saucepan</a> or <a href=\"handbook://block-aculinaryartillery:cauldron-iron\">cauldron</a> to concentrate it into fruit syrup.",
  
    "item-handbooktitle-dough": "Dough Enhancements (Expanded Foods)",
    "item-handbooktext-dough": "Flour can be mixed with water from a liquid container like usual, but <a href=\"handbook://kneadingmixingguide\">kneading</a> allows more ingredients to be combined together. In addition to water, milk, lard, and broth may be used to create dough. Optionally, lard/cooking oil and yeast starter can be added in. Adding any liquids beyond water will add small amounts of nutrition and healing to the finished bread product (including pies).",
}   
